  ],
  "slug": "immune-cells-3e89",
  "summary": "Public bulk and single-cell datasets are increasingly combined with genetic and proteomic data to nominate immune-cell-linked candidates.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these published studies is the integration of public bulk transcriptomes with single-cell profiling to localize disease signals to immune compartments. Monocyte and macrophage populations recur: a monocyte-enriched mitoxyperiosis-high endotype in ischemic stroke (CITE-seq, GSE285659), ANPEP as a monocyte-associated candidate in aortic dissection, Msr1 in Lyme arthritis, and senescence-associated monocyte/macrophage genes in pulmonary arterial hypertension. Other items extend the pattern to dendritic cells (AIF1 in IgA nephropathy), cDC2 expansion with macrophage depletion in COPD, and interferon-linked HIV restrictome activity. Several studies add genetic or proteomic layers, including proteome-wide Mendelian randomization in coronary artery disease and WGS with HLA loci in aortic dissection. The novelty lies mainly in candidate prioritization rather than mechanistic validation, and several items explicitly frame their findings as exploratory or hypothesis-generating.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-13"
}
